A request that looks in what was kept aside looks in the chunks of every bin.

The arenas give each chunk the bin of the first thing that is placed in it, and
a page of one slice is never placed in a chunk of another bin, to reduce
fragmentation. A piece that the embedder keeps aside (mi_keep_piece_aside()) has
sixteen slices, so what it leaves when it is given back lies in a chunk where no
small page is looked for: a request for a small block that cannot fail had the
embedder give back piece after piece and found none of them.

Where the search has found nothing and the request is one that looks again
(ask-who-may-take-address-space.patch), it goes through the chunks of all bins.
A search that finds room runs the same instructions as before, and no other
request looks anywhere else than before.

--- a/src/bitmap.c
+++ b/src/bitmap.c
@@ -1814,7 +1814,8 @@
   mi_assert_internal(MI_BFIELD_BITS >= MI_BCHUNK_FIELDS);
   const mi_bfield_t cmap_mask  = mi_bfield_mask(cmap_max_count,0);
   const size_t cmap_cycle      = cmap_acc+1;
-  const mi_chunkbin_t bbin = mi_chunkbin_of(n);
+  mi_chunkbin_t bbin = mi_chunkbin_of(n);
+again: ;
   // visit each cmap entry
   size_t cmap_idx = 0;
   mi_bfield_cycle_iterate(cmap_mask, tseq, cmap_cycle, cmap_idx, X)
@@ -1884,6 +1885,11 @@
     }
   }
   mi_bfield_cycle_iterate_end(X);
+  // what the embedder has let go of for a request that cannot fail lies in a chunk of the bin of the piece: such a request goes on to look in the chunks of every bin
+  if (bbin != MI_CBIN_HUGE && _mi_is_looking_in_room_kept_aside()) {
+    bbin = MI_CBIN_HUGE;
+    goto again;
+  }
   return false;
 }
 
